# Dodging: position_dodge

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()
mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()
diamonds = Giggly.Datasets.diamonds()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`position_dodge()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/position_dodge.html), which covers
`position_dodge2()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

Dodging keeps each mark's vertical position and sets the marks that share an `x` side by side.
`position_dodge()` dodges by group, so it needs the groups; `position_dodge2()` needs none, dodging
each mark by its own extent and leaving a little padding between them, which suits box plots of
different widths. `width` gives the width to dodge within, for marks narrower than the bars they sit
beside, and `preserve` keeps either the total width at each position or the width of each mark.
Giggly dodges any layer as `position_dodge()` does, with `position: :dodge` or `{:dodge, width:
width}`, marks with no width of their own, such as points, text and line ranges, only within a
width given, and marks with widths, such as bars, histograms, error bars and box plots, as
`position_dodge2()` does, with `position: :dodge2` or `{:dodge2, options}` and its `preserve:`,
`padding:` and `reverse:`, a position's options given in a tuple with its name where ggplot2 calls
its function. Not in Giggly yet: `position_dodge()`'s `preserve`,
`orientation` and `reverse` options, and `position_dodge2()`'s `width` option. The setup cell binds R's
`mtcars` and ggplot2's `mpg` and `diamonds`, `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0` and
`Giggly.Datasets.diamonds/0`, to their names in R.

## Dodging without groups

```r
ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) +
  geom_bar(position = "dodge2")
```

Giggly writes `factor(cyl)` as `{:factor, [:cyl]}` and `position = "dodge2"` as
`position: :dodge2`.

```elixir
Plot.new(mtcars, %{x: {:factor, [:cyl]}, fill: {:factor, [:vs]}})
|> Plot.geom_bar(position: :dodge2)
|> render.()
```

## The width of each bar

```r
# By default, dodging with `position_dodge2()` preserves the total width of
# the elements. You can choose to preserve the width of each element with:
ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) +
  geom_bar(position = position_dodge2(preserve = "single"))
```

`{:dodge2, options}` takes ggplot2's options, a word as an atom.

```elixir
Plot.new(mtcars, %{x: {:factor, [:cyl]}, fill: {:factor, [:vs]}})
|> Plot.geom_bar(position: {:dodge2, preserve: :single})
|> render.()
```

## Dodged histograms

```r
ggplot(diamonds, aes(price, fill = cut)) +
  geom_histogram(position="dodge2")
# see ?geom_bar for more examples
```

Giggly bins and dodges the diamonds with `Giggly.Plot.geom_histogram/3` and `position: :dodge2`,
and fills `cut`, an ordered factor, with viridis in the order of its levels, as R does.

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :price, fill: :cut})
|> Plot.geom_histogram(position: :dodge2)
|> render.()
```

## Frequency polygons instead

```r
# In this case a frequency polygon is probably a better choice
ggplot(diamonds, aes(price, colour = cut)) +
  geom_freqpoly()
```

```elixir
diamonds
|> Plot.new(%{x: :price, colour: :cut})
|> Plot.geom_freqpoly()
|> render.()
```

## Columns dodged by group

```r
# Dodging with various widths -------------------------------------
# To dodge items with different widths, you need to be explicit
df <- data.frame(
  x = c("a","a","b","b"),
  y = 2:5,
  g = rep(1:2, 2)
)
p <- ggplot(df, aes(x, y, group = g)) +
  geom_col(position = "dodge", fill = "grey50", colour = "black")
p
```

`rep(1:2, 2)` is `1, 2, 1, 2`, so each `x` has a column for each group. `position: :dodge` sets
the columns side by side within the width one column takes, and `fill:` and `colour:` fill and
border them. R's `"grey50"` has no name in CSS, so the fill is written as its hex, `"#7F7F7F"`.

```elixir
df = %{"x" => ["a", "a", "b", "b"], "y" => [2, 3, 4, 5], "g" => [1, 2, 1, 2]}

p =
  df
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :x, y: :y, group: :g})
  |> Plot.geom_col(position: :dodge, fill: "#7F7F7F", colour: "black")

render.(p)
```

## A line range has no width

```r
# A line range has no width:
p + geom_linerange(aes(ymin = y - 1, ymax = y + 1), position = "dodge")
```

With no width to dodge within, the line ranges are not moved apart: ggplot2 warns "Width not
defined" and draws them at the middle of each `x`, off their columns. Giggly refuses
`position: :dodge` for marks with no width of their own, saying why, as the cell shows. It writes
`y - 1` as `{&Kernel.-/2, [:y, 1]}` and `y + 1` as `{&Kernel.+/2, [:y, 1]}`.

```elixir
range = %{ymin: {&Kernel.-/2, [:y, 1]}, ymax: {&Kernel.+/2, [:y, 1]}}

try do
  Plot.geom_linerange(p, range, position: :dodge)
rescue
  error in NimbleOptions.ValidationError -> error.message
end
```

## Giving the width

```r
# So you must explicitly specify the width
p + geom_linerange(
  aes(ymin = y - 1, ymax = y + 1),
  position = position_dodge(width = 0.9)
)
```

Dodged within 0.9, the width the columns take, each line range sits on its column.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_linerange(range, position: {:dodge, width: 0.9})
|> render.()
```

## Error bars

```r
# The same principle applies to error bars, which are usually
# narrower than the bars
p + geom_errorbar(
  aes(ymin = y - 1, ymax = y + 1),
  width = 0.2,
  position = "dodge"
)
p + geom_errorbar(
  aes(ymin = y - 1, ymax = y + 1),
  width = 0.2,
  position = position_dodge(width = 0.9)
)
```

`"dodge"` sets the error bars side by side within their own width, 0.2, so they huddle at the
middle of each `x`; dodged within 0.9, each sits on its column.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_errorbar(range, width: 0.2, position: :dodge)
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_errorbar(range, width: 0.2, position: {:dodge, width: 0.9})
|> render.()
```

## Box plots

```r
# Box plots use position_dodge2 by default, and bars can use it too
ggplot(mpg, aes(factor(year), displ)) +
  geom_boxplot(aes(colour = hwy < 30))
```

Giggly writes `hwy < 30` as `{&Kernel.</2, [:hwy, 30]}`, which titles the legend `hwy < 30`, as R
does.

```elixir
Plot.new(mpg, %{x: {:factor, [:year]}, y: :displ})
|> Plot.geom_boxplot(%{colour: {&Kernel.</2, [:hwy, 30]}})
|> render.()
```

## Box plots of different widths

```r
ggplot(mpg, aes(factor(year), displ)) +
  geom_boxplot(aes(colour = hwy < 30), varwidth = TRUE)
```

`varwidth = TRUE` makes each box's width grow with the square root of its number of rows, and
`position_dodge2()` dodges the boxes by their own widths.

```elixir
Plot.new(mpg, %{x: {:factor, [:year]}, y: :displ})
|> Plot.geom_boxplot(%{colour: {&Kernel.</2, [:hwy, 30]}}, varwidth: true)
|> render.()
```

## Bars dodged the same way

```r
ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) +
  geom_bar(position = position_dodge2(preserve = "single"))

ggplot(mtcars, aes(factor(cyl), fill = factor(vs))) +
  geom_bar(position = position_dodge2(preserve = "total"))
```

Every 8-cylinder car has `vs` 0, so that `x` has one bar: as wide as one bar of a pair with
`preserve = "single"`, and as wide as the whole pair with `preserve = "total"`.

```elixir
Plot.new(mtcars, %{x: {:factor, [:cyl]}, fill: {:factor, [:vs]}})
|> Plot.geom_bar(position: {:dodge2, preserve: :single})
|> render.()
```

```elixir
Plot.new(mtcars, %{x: {:factor, [:cyl]}, fill: {:factor, [:vs]}})
|> Plot.geom_bar(position: {:dodge2, preserve: :total})
|> render.()
```
